  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1237 PM CDT Sun Sep 20 2026\n\nEasterly sfc flow is almost staving off movement of the rain past \nthe Missouri River this afternoon. There is a pocket of drier air at \nthe midlevels that is helping to hold the rain west river even with \nample llm in place. All of that effort will be for nought as the \nupper low slowly makes its way east through South Dakota tonight. As \nit sinks south and east Monday, the precip will exit with it. \n\nOnce again, the NBM is likely too high with its overnight lows given \nthe cloud cover and rain expected. All hi-res models keep lows in \nthe low to mid 50s (except in west central MN where some scattering \nis possible), but did not blend toward them today in deference to \nneighbors and because of the chance for scattered cloud cover in the \nnortheast. Expect clearing through the afternoon Monday from north \nto south as the system exits. \n\nReturn flow winds shift back to the southeast and south on Tuesday \nushering in some warmer air. Skies should be mostly clear which will \nalso help push temperatures above average. That said, models have \nshifted the upper level trends a bit for Tuesday moderating the \nupper ridge and leaving some remnant shortwave energy around \nwhich could increase clouds, especially along the Coteau. \nThere's now also a llj developing Tuesday night so can't rule \nout a shower or two early Wednesday. A 30 kt llj spreads over \nthe region Wednesday afternoon and increases Wednesday night. \nThere is some shortwave energy around, though it doesn't align \nperfectly. But, the ample mid level moisture should still allow \nfor some showers and thunderstorms to develop. A 700 mb low \ncould help amplify the precip probs through Friday night.",
